NNAAMMEE
       pnmtoddif - Convert a PNM image to DDIF format


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppnnmmttooddddiiff [--rreessoolluuttiioonn _x _y] [_p_n_m_f_i_l_e [_d_d_i_f_f_i_l_e]]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmttooddddiiff takes a PNM image and converts it into a DDIF image file.

       ppnnmmttooddddiiff  writes  PBM format (bitmap) data as a 1 bit DDIF, PGM format
       data (grayscale) as an 8 bit grayscale DDIF,  and  PPM  format  (color)
       data  as an 8,8,8 bit color DDIF.  ppnnmmttooddddiiff writes any DDIF image file
       uncompressed.  The data plane organization is interleaved by pixel.

       In addition to the number of pixels in the width and height  dimension,
       DDIF images also carry information about the size that the image should
       have, that is, the physical space that a pixel occupies.  Netpbm images
       do  not carry this information, hence you have to supply it externally.
       The default of 78 dpi has the beneficial property of not causing a  re‐
       size on most Digital Equipment Corporation color monitors.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppnnmmttooddddiiff recognizes  the
       following command line option:



       rreessoolluuttiioonn _x _y
              The  horizontal  and  vertical resolution of the output image in
              dots per inch.  Default is 78 dpi.




AARRGGUUMMEENNTTSS
       _p_n_m_f_i_l_e
              The filename for the image file in pnm format.   Default  is  to
              read from Standard Input.


       _d_d_i_f_f_i_l_e
              The  filename  for  the image file to be created in DDIF format.
              By default, ppnnmmttooddddiiff writes to Standard Output.




